## Prior art and positioning

Do not claim novelty of concept. Claim novelty of mechanism.

| Project | What it did | Why we differ |
|---|---|---|
| ENSFirewall (Open Agents) | Policies in ENS text records, enforced by ERC-4337 smart accounts | Policy in ENS, enforcement elsewhere. We put both in the naming layer. ENSv1. |
| WorkAgnt DelegateFlow (MetaMask) | Sub-agent delegation via ERC-7710, x402 payments | Token-based. We delete the token layer. |
| ACN | Agent subnames with skills/reputation, job board | Identity and discovery, no attenuation. ENSv1. |
| HumanENS | Agent subnames under a verified human, ENSIP-25 backlinks | Provenance, not permission. ENSv1. |
| AgentArena | Subname as agent registry, config in text records | Registry only. ENSv1. |
| AWS sample-agentic-delegation | Cedar attenuation, chain-hash tamper detection | Correct pattern, entirely offchain. |

**Say it this way:** every prior project in this space was built on ENSv1 primitives that could store policy but could not enforce it. Do **not** say the ENSv2 requirement "disqualifies" those teams — it disqualifies their existing submissions, not the teams, and someone could rebuild this week.

**Submission line:** "Storing agent policy in ENS has been done. Enforcing attenuation in the registry hierarchy itself has not."

---

## Language discipline

Judges have seen too many swarm projects.

- ❌ orchestrator, swarm, specialists, "agent hires other agents", multi-agent system
- ✅ attenuation, scope, delegation chain, revocation, permission tree, security primitive

Same code. Different reception.

Pick one root name and use it everywhere: **`attenuate.eth`**.

---

## The web view — one screen

Person C, day 5, half a day. If it takes longer, cut features, not the red flash.

- **Center: the tree.** Nodes are names, never addresses. Each shows capability chips and a remaining-budget bar. Node states: alive (solid), working (pulse), rejected (red flash), dead (greyed + struck through).
- **Right rail: transaction feed.** Every contract event with the tx hash linked to Sepolia Etherscan. This is proof-of-realness against a suspected mock.
- **Top: one input.** Type a name, see its subtree.
- **Bottom: three buttons.** Grant · Attempt Escalation · Revoke Root.
- **Node detail:** link to resolve the name in an external ENS client.

**Excluded:** login, settings, agent-creation wizard, charts, sidebar nav, dark-mode toggle.

**Never show a hex address anywhere.** Names only. The interface should argue ENS's case for them.

### Landing page — day 6 only, after the demo works

Single scroll: the claim in large type, a static tree image, three lines for the three guarantees, the metrics table, the two-sentence prior-art paragraph, and three buttons (Launch demo · GitHub · Video). No animations, no features grid, no roadmap, no team section. Host it at the ENS name so the page itself resolves through the thing you built.

