## ETHOnline rules compliance

Every rule from the official details page, and what we do about it.

### AI tools — read this carefully, it affects us directly

AI tools are permitted, with three conditions:

**Attribution.** Document in the submission where and how AI tools were used — which parts of the code, which specific files, which assets. → Ship an **`AI-USE.md`** listing this per directory. Not optional.

**Involvement.** AI should assist development, not create the entire project. Submissions relying entirely on AI without meaningful team contribution may not be eligible for partner prizes or finalist consideration. → Every person must have real authored commits. Do not let one person Claude-Code the whole repo.

**Spec-driven development.** Permitted, but if you use it you must include all spec files, prompts and planning artifacts in the submission repo. Judges need to see how you directed the AI, not just the output. → **Commit `SPEC.md` and a `prompts/` directory to the repo.** This is a requirement, not a nice-to-have. Our spec-driven workflow becomes an asset once it is visible.

### Start Fresh (Classic Track)

All work must begin after the hackathon started. No prior project-specific code, designs or assets except public libraries and starter kits. Projects built before the event can participate but are **not eligible for partner prizes or the Finalist category**.

→ Nothing predates Sep 4. Public libraries are fine; declare them.

### Version control

Large single commits or missing history may be disqualified. → Small, frequent, daily commits from all three people.

### Include everything

GitHub repo or equivalent proving the work was done during the hackathon. Clearly distinguish new work from reused. → README section separating our code from libraries.

### Partner prize submission

Up to 3 partner prizes. If a partner has multiple tracks you are eligible for all of them while it counts as one selection. **For each partner prize you must explain how you used or integrated their tools, provide feedback, and share comments.** This is the only way partners assess the project.

→ Write three separate write-ups in advance, not in the final hour:
- **Ledger** — Key Ring integration, remote enrollment, three device touchpoints, DX feedback
- **ENS** — every ENSv2 feature used and why, plus beta feedback and any issues filed
- **The Graph** — own subgraph, MCP server, Substreams, x402, benchmark numbers

### Judging criteria — five categories

| Criterion | Our answer |
|---|---|
| **Technicality** | Onchain attenuation invariant, O(1) epoch subtree revocation, allocation-based budget conservation |
| **Originality** | Prior work stored agent policy in ENS; enforcing attenuation in the registry hierarchy is new |
| **Practicality** | Working agent managing a real position on Sepolia, usable today |
| **Usability (UI/UX/DX)** | One-screen tree, names never hex addresses, MCP server usable standalone, `SKILL.md` |
| **WOW Factor** | Revoke one name, an entire subtree dies mid-execution in a single transaction |

**Usability includes DX.** The MCP server and SKILL.md are scored here, not just as a Graph deliverable.

### Async judging structure

Two rounds. Round one is asynchronous and screens projects; typically only the top 20% advance to live judging. Round one has **no impact on partner prize eligibility** and partners do not see those results. At most async events the majority of prizes go to projects that do **not** advance.

→ Optimise for partner prizes. Finalist is upside, not the plan.

### If we opt into Finalist judging

7 minutes total: **4 minutes demo, 3 minutes Q&A.** Prepare answers for: what inspired the project, what tools we used and why, what challenges we solved and how.

